      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "title: \"Self-Attention\"\n",
        "subtitle: \"Contextual token representations, permutation equivariance, and multi-head structure\"\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.chapter-opening}\n",
        "Self-attention is the operation that made the Transformer architecture possible.  In an RNN, information moves through a time-indexed hidden state.  In self-attention, all token positions in a sequence compare themselves with all other token positions directly.  The result is a new representation for each token, called a **contextual embedding**, whose meaning depends on the entire sequence.\n",
        "\n",
        "The mathematical object in this chapter is the self-attention map\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X \\mapsto \\operatorname{softmax}_{\\text{row}}\\left(\\frac{XW_QW_K^TX^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}+M\\right)XW_V.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This formula is compact, but it hides several important ideas: learned pairwise similarity, row-stochastic averaging, masking, positional information, permutation equivariance, and parallel attention heads.  We study each idea carefully.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Learning goals\n",
        "\n",
        "After studying this chapter, you should be able to:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. define self-attention as attention with $Q,K,V$ all computed from the same sequence matrix $X$;\n",
        "2. check every matrix dimension in a self-attention layer;\n",
        "3. explain why self-attention produces contextual token embeddings;\n",
        "4. prove the permutation equivariance of self-attention without positional encodings;\n",
        "5. explain why positional information is necessary for language modeling;\n",
        "6. construct padding masks and causal masks;\n",
        "7. interpret multi-head attention as several learned comparison geometries in parallel;\n",
        "8. implement single-head and multi-head self-attention from scratch in NumPy;\n",
        "9. use attention heatmaps carefully without over-interpreting them;\n",
        "10. explain the computational cost of self-attention.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 14.1 From attention to self-attention\n",
        "\n",
        "In Chapter 13, attention was defined for three matrices\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Q\\in R^{T_q\\times d_k},\\qquad K\\in R^{T_k\\times d_k},\\qquad V\\in R^{T_k\\times d_v},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "by\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{Attention}(Q,K,V)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\operatorname{softmax}_{\\text{row}}\\left(\\frac{QK^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right)V.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "In **self-attention**, the queries, keys, and values are all computed from the same sequence.  Let\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X\\in R^{T\\times d_{\\mathrm{model}}}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "be the matrix of token representations for a sequence of length $T$.  The $i$th row $x_i^T$ represents token position $i$.  A self-attention head uses learned matrices\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "W_Q\\in R^{d_{\\mathrm{model}}\\times d_k},\\qquad\n",
        "W_K\\in R^{d_{\\mathrm{model}}\\times d_k},\\qquad\n",
        "W_V\\in R^{d_{\\mathrm{model}}\\times d_v}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "to form\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Q=XW_Q,\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "K=XW_K,\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "V=XW_V.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Z\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\operatorname{SelfAttn}(X)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\operatorname{softmax}_{\\text{row}}\\left(\\frac{XW_QW_K^TX^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right)XW_V.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The output $Z\\in R^{T\\times d_v}$ has one row for each token position.  Each row is a weighted average of transformed value vectors from the same sequence.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**One sentence summary.**  Self-attention lets every token position build a new representation by asking: which other token positions in this same sequence are relevant to me?\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 14.2 Token matrices and dimension bookkeeping\n",
        "\n",
        "A sequence of tokens\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "w_1,w_2,\\ldots,w_T\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "is first mapped to vectors.  If $E\\in R^{|V|\\times d_{\\mathrm{model}}}$ is a token embedding matrix and token $w_i$ has integer index $r_i$, then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "e_i=E_{r_i,:}\\in R^{d_{\\mathrm{model}}}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Usually we also add positional information $p_i\\in R^{d_{\\mathrm{model}}}$:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "x_i=e_i+p_i.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Stacking rows gives\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X=\n",
        "\\begin{bmatrix}\n",
        "x_1^T\\\\\n",
        "x_2^T\\\\\n",
        "\\vdots\\\\\n",
        "x_T^T\n",
        "\\end{bmatrix}\n",
        "\\in R^{T\\times d_{\\mathrm{model}}}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For one attention head:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "XW_Q\\in R^{T\\times d_k},\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "XW_K\\in R^{T\\times d_k},\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "XW_V\\in R^{T\\times d_v}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The score matrix is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "S=\\frac{QK^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\in R^{T\\times T}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The attention matrix is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "A=\\operatorname{softmax}_{\\text{row}}(S)\\in R^{T\\times T}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The output is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Z=AV\\in R^{T\\times d_v}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The row $z_i^T$ is the new representation of token position $i$.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 14.3 Contextual embeddings\n",
        "\n",
        "A static word embedding assigns one vector to each token type.  For example, the word `bank` might have a single embedding vector.  But the meaning of `bank` changes in different contexts:\n",
        "\n",
        "- `river bank`\n",
        "- `bank loan`\n",
        "- `central bank`\n",
        "\n",
        "Self-attention produces **contextual embeddings**.  The output vector $z_i$ at position $i$ depends not only on $x_i$, but also on all rows of $X$ through the attention weights.\n",
        "\n",
        "Row $i$ of the output is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "z_i\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\sum_{j=1}^T a_{ij}v_j,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "a_{ij}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{\\exp(q_i^Tk_j/\\sqrt{d_k})}\n",
        "{\\sum_{\\ell=1}^T \\exp(q_i^Tk_\\ell/\\sqrt{d_k})}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Since $q_i$, $k_j$, and $v_j$ are all functions of the input token representations, the vector $z_i$ changes when the surrounding sentence changes.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Contextualization.**  A token representation is contextual if changing another token in the same sequence can change the representation at the original position.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "### A small mathematical test for contextuality\n",
        "\n",
        "Let $F:R^{T\\times d}\\to R^{T\\times d_v}$ be a sequence model.  The representation at position $i$ is contextual if there exists some $j\\ne i$ such that\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\frac{\\partial F_i(X)}{\\partial x_j}\\ne 0.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For self-attention, this derivative is usually nonzero because the scores $q_i^Tk_j$ and the value $v_j$ both depend on $x_j$.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 14.4 Self-attention as a learned graph on tokens\n",
        "\n",
        "For a fixed input sequence $X$, the attention matrix\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "A(X)\\in R^{T\\times T}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "can be viewed as the weighted adjacency matrix of a directed graph on token positions.  The edge weight from query position $i$ to key/value position $j$ is $a_{ij}$.\n",
        "\n",
        "This graph is:\n",
        "\n",
        "- **weighted**, because $a_{ij}\\in[0,1]$;\n",
        "- **directed**, because usually $a_{ij}\\ne a_{ji}$;\n",
        "- **row-stochastic**, because $\\sum_j a_{ij}=1$;\n",
        "- **data-dependent**, because $A$ changes with $X$.\n",
        "\n",
        "Self-attention therefore constructs a sequence-specific graph and then performs a weighted message-passing step:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "z_i=\\sum_{j=1}^T a_{ij}v_j.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This connects self-attention with graph neural networks.  The difference is that the graph is not given externally; it is inferred from learned similarities inside the sequence.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 14.5 Permutation equivariance without positional information\n",
        "\n",
        "A subtle but important mathematical property of self-attention is permutation equivariance.  Without positional information or masks, self-attention does not know the order of tokens.  It only sees a set of input rows.\n",
        "\n",
        "Let $P\\in R^{T\\times T}$ be a permutation matrix.  The matrix $PX$ is obtained by permuting the rows of $X$.  Define one-head self-attention by\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "F(X)=\\operatorname{softmax}_{\\text{row}}\\left(\\frac{XW_QW_K^TX^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right)XW_V.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "F(PX)=PF(X).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This property is called **permutation equivariance**.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Proof\n",
        "\n",
        "Let\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Q=XW_Q,\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "K=XW_K,\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "V=XW_V.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For the permuted input $PX$,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Q'=PXW_Q=PQ,\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "K'=PXW_K=PK,\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "V'=PXW_V=PV.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The score matrix becomes\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "S'\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{Q'(K')^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{(PQ)(PK)^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{P Q K^T P^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\n",
        "=\n",
        "P S P^T.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Row-wise softmax commutes with simultaneous row and column permutation, so\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{softmax}_{\\text{row}}(S')\n",
        "=\n",
        "P\\operatorname{softmax}_{\\text{row}}(S)P^T\n",
        "=\n",
        "PAP^T.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "F(PX)\n",
        "=\n",
        "(PAP^T)(PV)\n",
        "=PA(P^TP)V\n",
        "=PAV\n",
        "=PF(X).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This proves the claim.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Why this matters\n",
        "\n",
        "A language model must distinguish\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "student studies model\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "from\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "model studies student\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "because the order changes the meaning.  Self-attention without positional information treats these as row permutations of the same collection of token vectors.  The output rows are permuted in the same way, but the model has no intrinsic sense of first, second, or third position.\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore Transformers add positional information.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 14.6 Positional information breaks pure permutation symmetry\n",
        "\n",
        "A common input representation is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "x_i=e(w_i)+p_i,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $e(w_i)$ is the token embedding and $p_i$ is a position vector.  The sequence matrix is then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X=\n",
        "\\begin{bmatrix}\n",
        "(e(w_1)+p_1)^T\\\\\n",
        "\\cdots\\\\\n",
        "(e(w_T)+p_T)^T\n",
        "\\end{bmatrix}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Now token identity and token position are mixed in each row.  The model can learn different behaviors for early positions, nearby positions, or relative patterns.\n",
        "\n",
        "There are several common approaches:\n",
        "\n",
        "- learned absolute position embeddings;\n",
        "- sinusoidal position encodings;\n",
        "- relative position biases;\n",
        "- rotary position embeddings.\n",
        "\n",
        "Chapter 16 studies these in more detail.  For now, the key point is simple: self-attention provides token-token interaction, but positional information provides order.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 14.7 Masked self-attention\n",
        "\n",
        "A mask is added to the score matrix before softmax:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "A\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\operatorname{softmax}_{\\text{row}}\n",
        "\\left(\n",
        "\\frac{QK^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}+M\n",
        "\\right).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Allowed entries of $M$ are $0$.  Disallowed entries are large negative numbers.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Padding mask\n",
        "\n",
        "A padding mask prevents real tokens from attending to artificial padding tokens.  If token position $j$ is padding, then set\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "M_{ij}=-10^9\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "for every query position $i$.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Causal mask\n",
        "\n",
        "A causal mask prevents position $i$ from attending to future positions $j>i$:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "M_{ij}=\\begin{cases}\n",
        "0, & j\\le i,\\\\\n",
        "-10^9, & j>i.\n",
        "\\end{cases}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Causal masking is necessary for autoregressive language modeling.  When predicting token $w_{t+1}$, the representation at position $t$ must not use information from tokens after $t$.\n",
        "\n",
        "The causal factorization is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(w_1,\\ldots,w_T)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\prod_{t=1}^T P(w_t\\mid w_1,\\ldots,w_{t-1}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Causal self-attention enforces this conditional structure inside the network.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 14.8 Multi-head self-attention\n",
        "\n",
        "A single attention head uses one learned query-key geometry.  Multi-head attention uses $H$ such heads in parallel.\n",
        "\n",
        "For head $h$, define\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Q^{(h)}=XW_Q^{(h)},\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "K^{(h)}=XW_K^{(h)},\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "V^{(h)}=XW_V^{(h)}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The head output is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Z^{(h)}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\operatorname{softmax}_{\\text{row}}\n",
        "\\left(\n",
        "\\frac{Q^{(h)}(K^{(h)})^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}+M\n",
        "\\right)V^{(h)}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The head outputs are concatenated:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Z_{\\mathrm{cat}}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\operatorname{Concat}(Z^{(1)},\\ldots,Z^{(H)})\n",
        "\\in R^{T\\times Hd_v}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Then an output projection maps back to the model dimension:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{MHA}(X)\n",
        "=\n",
        "Z_{\\mathrm{cat}}W_O,\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "W_O\\in R^{Hd_v\\times d_{\\mathrm{model}}}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "If $d_v=d_k=d_{\\mathrm{model}}/H$, then the concatenated output has dimension $d_{\\mathrm{model}}$, which is convenient for residual connections.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Multi-head viewpoint.**  Each head learns a different similarity geometry.  One head may emphasize nearby tokens, another may emphasize matching syntax, and another may emphasize semantic roles.  The architecture does not force these roles; they emerge, if useful, from the training objective.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 14.9 Self-attention as matrix-valued feature construction\n",
        "\n",
        "From a statistical learning point of view, a self-attention layer is a data-dependent feature map.\n",
        "\n",
        "For a fixed parameter collection $\\theta=(W_Q,W_K,W_V)$,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "F_\\theta(X)=A_\\theta(X)XW_V,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "A_\\theta(X)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\operatorname{softmax}_{\\text{row}}\n",
        "\\left(\\frac{XW_QW_K^TX^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is nonlinear in $X$ because $A_\\theta(X)$ itself depends on $X$.  However, conditional on the attention matrix $A_\\theta(X)$, the output is linear in the values:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Z=A_\\theta(X)V.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "So self-attention has two layers of structure:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. construct a similarity matrix from $X$;\n",
        "2. use that matrix to average transformed vectors.\n",
        "\n",
        "This decomposition is often the cleanest way to understand the operation.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 14.10 Attention entropy and sharpness\n",
        "\n",
        "For each query position $i$, the attention row $a_i=(a_{i1},\\ldots,a_{iT})$ is a probability vector.  Its entropy is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "H(a_i)=-\\sum_{j=1}^T a_{ij}\\log a_{ij}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Low entropy means the query attends mostly to a few positions.  High entropy means the query averages broadly across the sequence.\n",
        "\n",
        "If the scores are multiplied by a scale parameter $\\gamma>0$,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "a_{ij}(\\gamma)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{\\exp(\\gamma s_{ij})}{\\sum_{\\ell=1}^T \\exp(\\gamma s_{i\\ell})},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "then increasing $\\gamma$ usually makes attention sharper.  Decreasing $\\gamma$ makes attention more uniform.\n",
        "\n",
        "This is one reason the factor $1/\\sqrt{d_k}$ matters: it controls the typical scale of the scores before the softmax.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 14.11 Computational complexity\n",
        "\n",
        "For sequence length $T$ and key dimension $d_k$, the score matrix\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "QK^T\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "requires roughly $O(T^2d_k)$ arithmetic operations and stores $O(T^2)$ attention weights.  The multiplication\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "AV\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "requires roughly $O(T^2d_v)$ operations.\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus standard full self-attention scales quadratically in sequence length.  This is powerful for moderate sequences but expensive for very long documents.  Many efficient Transformer variants modify the attention pattern to reduce this cost.\n",
        "\n",
        "For this book, the main mathematical point is:\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "The power of full self-attention comes from all-pairs token interaction.  The cost of full self-attention also comes from all-pairs token interaction.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 14.12 Worked example: two query positions\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose a sequence has three value vectors\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "v_1=(1,0),\\qquad v_2=(0,1),\\qquad v_3=(1,1).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Assume that after softmax the first two attention rows are\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "a_1=(0.7,0.2,0.1),\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "a_2=(0.1,0.3,0.6).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "z_1=0.7v_1+0.2v_2+0.1v_3\n",
        "=(0.8,0.3),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "z_2=0.1v_1+0.3v_2+0.6v_3\n",
        "=(0.7,0.9).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The same value vectors produce different contextual embeddings because the attention rows are different.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 14.13 Python example: single-head self-attention from scratch\n",
        "\n",
        "The next code block implements one self-attention head using NumPy."
